---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { currentTag, latestReleaseUrl, site } from '../../data/site';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (path = '') => `${base}${path}`;
---
<Layout current="install" title={`Install StackPilot ${currentTag} — StackPilot`}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-kicker">Installation · {currentTag}</div>
      <h1>Install StackPilot.</h1>
      <p>The current stable release is {currentTag}. The release installers download the native binary and bundled recipe library so StackPilot works from any normal project directory.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content:flex-start;margin-top:22px">
        <a class="button" href={latestReleaseUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View {currentTag} on GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell docs-layout">
    <aside class="docs-nav">
      <a class="active" href="#unix">macOS / Linux</a>
      <a href="#windows">Windows</a>
      <a href="#verify">Verify</a>
      <a href="#support">Release support</a>
      <a href="#source">From source</a>
      <a href={href('docs/')}>CLI docs →</a>
    </aside>
    <article class="doc">
      <h2 id="unix">macOS & Linux</h2>
      <p>The install script detects your OS and architecture, downloads the latest GitHub release, installs <code>stackpilot</code> into <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and installs the bundled recipes beside the executable.</p>
      <div class="code-block"><button class="copy-button" data-copy="unix-code">Copy</button><pre id="unix-code"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gODtECH-Ctl-Create/StackPilot/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre></div>
      <div class="note">Published native packages currently cover Linux x86_64, macOS x86_64 and macOS Apple Silicon. Linux ARM64 is not published yet.</div>

      <h2 id="windows">Windows PowerShell</h2>
      <p>Run PowerShell as your normal user. The installer downloads the Windows x86_64 archive, installs <code>stackpilot.exe</code> and its recipes into your user profile, and updates your user PATH.</p>
      <div class="code-block"><button class="copy-button" data-copy="win-code">Copy</button><pre id="win-code"><code>irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gODtECH-Ctl-Create/StackPilot/main/scripts/install.ps1 | iex</code></pre></div>
      <div class="note">The {currentTag} Windows path has been verified end-to-end from installation through diagnostics, recipe discovery, planning and real NestJS project generation.</div>

      <h2 id="verify">Verify the installation</h2>
      <div class="code-block"><button class="copy-button" data-copy="verify-code">Copy</button><pre id="verify-code"><code>stackpilot --version
stackpilot doctor
stackpilot recipes
stackpilot plan smoke-test --recipe base --non-interactive</code></pre></div>
      <p><code>stackpilot --version</code> should report <strong>stackpilot {site.currentVersion}</strong>. <code>doctor</code> should validate the bundled recipe directory, and <code>recipes</code> should list the <code>base</code> recipe.</p>
      <p>Then create your first backend service:</p>
      <div class="code-block"><button class="copy-button" data-copy="first-code">Copy</button><pre id="first-code"><code>stackpilot new my-service --language Rust</code></pre></div>

      <h2 id="support">Release support</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Architecture</th><th>Release package</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Windows</td><td>x86_64</td><td><span class="tag">published</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Linux</td><td>x86_64</td><td><span class="tag">published</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>macOS</td><td>x86_64</td><td><span class="tag">published</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>macOS</td><td>Apple Silicon</td><td><span class="tag">published</span></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="source">Install from source</h2>
      <p>If you already have a Rust toolchain, Cargo can install StackPilot directly from the repository. Release installers are recommended for normal users because they also install the recipe library required by the CLI.</p>
      <div class="code-block"><button class="copy-button" data-copy="source-code">Copy</button><pre id="source-code"><code>cargo install --git https://github.com/gODtECH-Ctl-Create/StackPilot.git</code></pre></div>
      <p><a href={href('docs/')}>Continue to the CLI guide →</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
